.TH HTTPD 8
.SH NAME
httpd \- web server for a name space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B svc/httpd/httpd
[
.B -Lv
] [
.BI -a " addr"
] [
.BI -c " cert"
.BI -k " key"
] [
.BI -l " log"
] [
.BI -p " path"
]... [
.BI -w " wikibase"
] [
.BI -T " type"
] [
.BI -r " httpsport"
] [
.BI -S " secs"
] [
.BI -C " maxage"
] [
.BI -m " maxlog"
] [
.BI -M " oldlogs"
] [
.BI -A " clientcas"
] [
.BI -P " passwords"
] [
.BI -x " rules"
] [
.BI -n " maxconns"
] [
.BI -s " persource"
] [
.BI -b " maxbody"
]
.I root
.PP
.B svc/httpd/passwd
[
.BI -i " iterations"
]
.I user
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Httpd
serves the directory
.I root
over HTTP/1.1, and over TLS 1.3 when given a certificate and key.
What it serves is whatever is in
.I root
when a request arrives, including what has been bound or mounted under it:
a device's file server mounted at
.IB root /gps
is a set of pages whose
.B GET
reads the device.
Dynamic content is a file server's business, not the web server's;
there is no CGI.
.PP
Each connection runs in a name space of its own, made from the server's
as it was started:
.B pctl(FORKNS)
(see
.IR sys-pctl (2)),
.I root
bound over
.B /
with
.BR MREPL ,
then
.BR pctl(NODEVS) ,
an empty environment
.RB ( NEWENV )
and no descriptors but the connection's, the clock's and the TLS
module's entropy source
.RB ( NEWFD ).
A connection does not write the log: it sends its line to the
listener, which alone holds the log.
In that name space
.B /
is the site and
.B ..
from it is the site; the server's own
.BR /dis ,
.BR /dev ,
.BR /prog
and the rest are not there, and no device can be attached by name.
In the hosted emulator, a host symbolic link in the site is not
followed once
.B NODEVS
is set
.RI ( fs (3)).
The name space sketch:
.IP
.EX
/		root, as it was when httpd started
/index.html	served for /
/gps/	...	a mount made under root before httpd started
/wiki/	...	wikifs, for -w /wiki
.EE
.PP
Requests are refused before they reach the name space when they are
not strictly HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/1.0: a request line or header line longer
than 8192 bytes (414, 431), more than 64 headers or 16384 bytes of them
(431), a folded header, a line ending in a bare LF or holding a bare CR
or NUL, a control character in a header, a header name that is not a
token, no
.B Host
under HTTP/1.1 or more than one, a
.B Host
that is not a host and port (400);
.B Transfer-Encoding
(501: no request body taken needs it, and with
.B Content-Length
it is how requests are smuggled), two
.B Content-Length
headers that differ, or one that is not digits (400).
A path is percent-decoded once, as UTF-8; a NUL, a control character,
bytes that are not UTF-8, a
.B .
or
.B ..
segment (a browser removes them before it sends) or a path longer than
2048 bytes is refused (400).
A path with a segment beginning with
.B .
is not found (404).
A refused request ends its connection.
.PP
.B GET
and
.B HEAD
send a file with its
.BR Content-Type
(by its name's extension; for a name with none,
.BR -T 's
type),
.BR ETag
(from its qid),
.BR Last-Modified ,
and
.BR "X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff" ;
.B If-None-Match
and
.B If-Modified-Since
are answered with 304, a single byte range with 206
.RB ( If-Range
honoured; several ranges get the whole file).
A file whose length is 0 in its stat (a device's status file, a
stream) is read until end of file and sent chunked, with
.BR "Cache-Control: no-store" .
A file with a compressed copy beside it,
.IB name .gz
(made ahead of time, as with
.BR "gzip -9 -k" ),
is sent as that copy, with
.BR "Content-Encoding: gzip" ,
to a client whose
.B Accept-Encoding
takes gzip, and as itself to one that does not; both answers carry
.BR "Vary: Accept-Encoding" .
Nothing is compressed as it is sent.
.PP
A file asked for with an
.B Accept
naming
.B text/event-stream
(as a browser's
.B EventSource
asks) is sent as Server-Sent Events: each read of the file is an event,
each line of it a
.B data:
line.
A device's event file, whose read waits for a change (gpsfs's
.BR event ,
the theme's
.BR event ),
is then a live page: the reads have no deadline, and after 15 seconds
with nothing to send a comment line is sent, which keeps the stream
open through proxies and finds a client that has gone.
The file ending ends the stream; an
.B EventSource
then opens it again.
At most half of
.I maxconns
connections hold event streams, and half of
.I persource
from one address; one more is refused (503).
The same file asked for otherwise is read as any other, under the
deadline below.
A directory is sent as its
.B index.html
if it has one; otherwise it is refused (403), or listed with
.BR -L .
A directory named without its final
.B /
is redirected (301) to the name with it.
Pages the server makes itself (errors and listings) carry a
.B Content-Security-Policy
that loads and runs nothing.
.PP
.B POST
is taken only by the paths named with
.BR -p ,
whose body (at most
.I maxbody
bytes, default 65536, with a
.BR Content-Length )
replaces the file's contents in one write, as the shell's
.B >
does (a file that cannot be truncated, a device's
.BR ctl ,
is written as it is).
If the file can then be read, what it gives is the answer (200);
otherwise there is none (204).
With
.BR -w ,
.B /magic/wikipost
takes the edit form of a
.I wikifs
.RB ( appl/cmd/wikifs )
mounted at
.I wikibase
in the site, writes the page to
.IB wikibase /new
and redirects (303) to the page; the form's own
.B base
field is not followed.
Either is refused (403) when the request comes from another site: an
.B Origin
that is not this server's, or a
.B Sec-Fetch-Site
that is not
.B same-origin
or
.BR none .
A request with neither (not from a browser) is taken.
Who may write is the business of the next section: without it a
.B -p
path is anyone's who can reach the server.
.SS Who may
.I Httpd
takes two proofs of who a client is, over TLS only (it will not start
with
.B -A
or
.B -P
and no
.BR -c ):
.TP
Client certificates
With
.BI -A " clientcas" \fR,
a PEM file of CA certificates (and, if any, their CRLs as
.B "X509 CRL"
blocks), the TLS handshake asks every client for a certificate those
CAs issued, naming them, so a browser offers only those.
A client that gives one must prove its key and its chain must end at
one of the CAs: signatures, validity, basicConstraints, keyUsage and
path length checked, at most 64 signatures in the search for a path;
the leaf's extended key usage, if it has one, must name client
authentication (a server's certificate from the same CA is refused),
and a CRL from the file must not list it (a CRL past its next update
still counts).
A CRL no CA in the file signed stops
.I httpd
starting.
A client that gives none goes on without one.
A refused certificate ends the handshake.
Revoke a certificate by a CRL in the file, or by its name leaving the
rules.
.TP
Passwords
With
.BI -P " passwords" \fR,
a file of lines that
.B svc/httpd/passwd
makes, HTTP Basic authentication:
.IB user :pbkdf2-sha256: iterations : salt : hash \fR,
the hash PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256 (RFC 8018) of the password, 100000
iterations unless
.B -i
says.
.B Svc/httpd/passwd
reads the password from its standard input.
The file is read again when it changes.
Passwords are checked by one process, one at a time; an unknown user
costs as much as a known one, so a name is not learnt by the time; a
password found right is taken again for five minutes without its hash.
Ten wrong passwords from one network (an IPv4 address, an IPv6 /64)
in ten minutes, or fifty for one user from anywhere, and further
attempts from it, or for them, are refused (429) for the rest of the
ten minutes, except a password already found right.
.PP
.BI -x " rules"
says which paths need which.
A line is
.RB [ GET | POST ]
.I path term ...
(a rule for GET is HEAD's too; one with neither method both), or
.B realm
.I words
(the name a browser shows when it asks for a password).
A term is
.B none
(anyone),
.B password
(any user),
.BI password= user\fR,
.B cert
(any certificate from the CAs),
.BI cert= cn
(one whose subject's common name is
.IR cn )
or
.BI cert=sha256: hex
(the one whose DER's SHA-256 is that); a request may have the path if
it meets any one of its rule's terms.
A request's rule is the deepest whose path is its path or a directory
of it, a rule for its method before one for both.
Paths are compared in lower case, and a rule for a path that existed
when
.I httpd
started matches by that file's identity too (its qid and device, by a
stat of each directory of the request's path): a host that takes
.B /ADMIN
or
.B /ADMINI~1
for
.B /admin
(macOS, Windows), or folds a name's other letters, opens the same
directory and meets the same rule.
A path no rule names is refused (403).
Every file a request may be answered from is checked: a
.IB name .gz
copy by
.IR name 's
rule as well as its own, a directory's
.B index.html
by its own rule, and
.B /magic/wikipost
by the POST rule of
.I wikibase
and
.IB wikibase /new\fR.
Without
.BR -x :
with
.B -P
every request needs a password or a certificate; with
.B -A
a POST needs a certificate.
.PP
The checks come before anything of the site is opened, so a refusal
says nothing of what is there: 401 with
.B WWW-Authenticate
when a password would do, else 403.
While anything is protected a path with a segment like
.B ~1
(a Windows short name) is refused (400), as always is one with a
segment ending in a dot or blank, a backslash, or a Windows device's
name
.RB ( con ,
.BR nul ,
.BR com1 ...).
A listing leaves out what its client may not have.
The log's user field is the user, or
.BI cert: cn\fR.
.PP
Each phase of a connection has a deadline: 10 seconds for the TLS
handshake, 10 for the first request's headers, 15 between requests
and for their headers, and 30 for each read of a body, write of a
response and read of a file (but an event stream's), and 30 for the
whole of a request's body.
A connection past one is hung up and its processes killed by the
listener, which holds the connections' control files; a file server that
never answers holds a connection only that long.
The listener admits at most
.I maxconns
connections at once (default 64), and at most
.I persource
from one address (default 16); a connection past either is closed
unanswered.
A connection is closed after 1000 requests.
.PP
The time in
.B Date
headers and the log is read from
.BR /dev/time ,
opened before the connections are confined, so a clock set after
.I httpd
started (by
.IR timesync (8)
or NTP on a board that boots without one) is followed.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.BI -a " addr"
Announce
.I addr
(default
.B tcp!*!443
with TLS,
.B tcp!*!80
without).
.TP
.BI -c " cert" " -k" " key"
Serve TLS 1.3 alone
.RB ( module/tls.m 's
server: the X25519MLKEM768 hybrid first, then X25519, then P-256 by
HelloRetryRequest; of AES-128-GCM, AES-256-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305, the
one fastest on this machine that the client offers, timed when
.I httpd
starts (clients put AES-GCM first, being fast where their processor
has AES instructions; where this one has none, ChaCha20-Poly1305 is
several times faster); ALPN
.BR http/1.1 ;
no resumption or early data).
.I Cert
is a PEM chain, leaf first, whose key is ECDSA P-256;
.I key
is its PEM private key, SEC1
.RB ( "EC PRIVATE KEY" )
or PKCS #8
.RB ( "PRIVATE KEY" ).
.I Httpd
will not start if the key is not the certificate's.
Clients that offer only TLS 1.2 or earlier are refused.
.TP
.BI -l " log"
Append each request to
.I log
in Common Log Format with the referer and agent (Apache's
.IR combined );
the default is standard output.
The log is rotated when it reaches
.I maxlog
bytes (default
.BR 1m ):
.IB log . oldlogs
is removed, each
.IB log . i
becomes
.IB log . i+1\fR,
.I log
becomes
.IB log .1
and a new
.I log
is begun, so it takes at most about
.RI ( oldlogs +1)
times
.I maxlog
of storage.
.TP
.BI -m " maxlog"
The size at which the log is rotated: bytes, or with
.B k
or
.B m
after it;
.B 0
never rotates it.
.TP
.BI -M " oldlogs"
How many old logs are kept (default 2).
A file server that cannot rename a file has its log truncated instead.
.TP
.B -L
List directories that have no
.BR index.html .
.TP
.BI -p " path"
Take
.B POST
at
.I path
in the site.
.TP
.BI -w " wikibase"
Take wikifs's edit form at
.BR /magic/wikipost .
.TP
.BI -T " type"
The type of a file whose name has no extension (default
.BR application/octet-stream ).
A site whose pages are named so, as
.IR wercfs (4)
names them, is served with
.BR "-T text/html" .
.TP
.BI -r " httpsport"
Answer every request with a redirect (301) to the same place on
.B https
at
.I httpsport
of the request's host: a plain server in front of a TLS one.
.TP
.BI -S " secs"
Send
.B Strict-Transport-Security
with
.BI max-age= secs
(TLS only).
Browsers then refuse the site's plain address, and a certificate they do
not trust, for that long: give it only for a certificate they trust.
.TP
.BI -C " maxage"
Send
.BI "Cache-Control: max-age=" maxage
with files that are not HTML pages: a browser keeps them that long
without asking.
Pages are asked about each time (their
.B ETag
answers 304), so a changed site is seen at once; give an asset a new
name when it changes.
.TP
.BI -n " maxconns" ", -s" " persource" ", -b" " maxbody"
The limits above.
.TP
.B -v
Report refused requests, failed handshakes and connections hung up,
and the TLS suites in the order timed, on standard error.
.TP
.BI -A " clientcas" ", -P" " passwords" ", -x" " rules"
Client certificates, passwords and the rules, above.
.SH EXAMPLES
A device's pages over TLS, with its sensor's file server in the site
and its LED's control file writable:
.IP
.EX
mount {gpsfs} /services/www/gps
bind /dev/led /services/www/led
svc/httpd/httpd -c /keys/web.pem -k /keys/web.key -p /led \e
	-l /services/logs/httpd /services/www &
svc/httpd/httpd -r 443 /services/www &
.EE
.PP
A page that follows the position as it changes, through gpsfs's
.B event
file as an event stream (the page's script, served from the site):
.IP
.EX
new EventSource("/gps/event").onmessage =
	e => document.getElementById("fix").textContent = e.data;
.EE
.PP
A wiki, browsed at
.B /wiki/
and edited from its pages:
.IP
.EX
mount {wikifs -d /services/wiki} /services/www/wiki
svc/httpd/httpd -w /wiki /services/www
.EE
.PP
A werc site, its pages made by
.IR wercfs (4):
.IP
.EX
mount {wercfs /services/werc/sites/example.org} /services/www
svc/httpd/httpd -T text/html /services/www
.EE
.PP
A certificate and key OpenSSL makes for it:
.IP
.EX
openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -nodes \e
	-keyout web.key -out web.pem -days 365 -subj /CN=device.example \e
	-addext subjectAltName=DNS:device.example
.EE
.PP
A device's administration by certificate, its pages by password, its
public pages open:
.IP
.EX
cp ca.pem /keys/clientcas
echo secret | svc/httpd/passwd alice > /keys/passwords
echo 'realm The device' > /keys/rules
echo '/		password cert' >> /keys/rules
echo '/public	none' >> /keys/rules
echo '/admin	cert=ops-laptop' >> /keys/rules
echo 'POST /led	cert' >> /keys/rules
svc/httpd/httpd -c /keys/web.pem -k /keys/web.key -A /keys/clientcas \e
	-P /keys/passwords -x /keys/rules -p /led/ctl /services/www &
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /dis/svc/httpd/httpd.dis
.TP
.B /dis/svc/httpd/httpd.dis
.TP
.B /dis/svc/httpd/passwd.dis
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/svc/httpd/httpd.b
.br
